1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,350
What if it never stopped getting darker?

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,080
It's getting darker.

3
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:10,920
An overcast day is about 1,000 lux. A hundred times dimmer than sunlight.

4
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:12,920
The streetlights flicker on.

5
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:16,840
When dusk ends, the light is about 3.4 lux.

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,680
The colors drain away.

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:23,800
In dim light, your eyes switch to rod cells. Rods can't see color.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,560
Only the moon is left.

9
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:31,800
A full moon gives about 0.25 lux. The Sun is 400,000 times brighter.

10
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,000
The streetlights go out. One by one.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,240
Something moves between the trees.

12
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:42,040
Many animals have a mirror behind the retina. It's why their eyes glow at night.

13
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,640
The moon is gone.

14
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:48,920
A moonless night, lit only by stars: about 0.002 lux.

15
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,360
The stars go out.

16
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:54,840
A cloudy night with no moon: about 0.0001 lux.

17
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,680
You can't see your own hand.

18
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:01,000
In total darkness, the brain starts inventing shapes that aren't there.

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,760
But some of them are real.

20
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:06,440
A cat can see in light about six times dimmer than you can.

21
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,440
And it can see you.

22
00:01:10,372 --> 00:01:12,392
Wait.

23
00:01:12,892 --> 00:01:15,992
What if it never stopped getting quieter?

24
00:01:16,592 --> 00:01:19,750
What if it never stopped getting darker?
